<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:cards="using:TanMenu.Wpf.Views.Settings"
        x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.Dialogs.OptionsWindow"
        Width="711" Height="479"
        CanResize="False"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceBaseBrush}">

  <!--
    ══ THE OPTIONS DIALOG — a Windows 7 property sheet ═══════════════════════════════════════════
    Round 8, Task 2. The settings surface was a PAGE inside the shell (Views/SettingsPage.axaml,
    deleted in the same commit) and is a modal dialog now. Everything below is PUBLISHED unless it
    says otherwise, and every published sentence is quoted rather than paraphrased.

    MODAL, and not by preference.
    https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/win-property-win — "Options dialog boxes.
    Properties for an application are displayed in a modal dialog box." win-dialog-box then pairs
    modality strictly with the commit model: "Modal dialog boxes ... Use a delayed commit model",
    "Don't use OK buttons in modeless dialog boxes", "Don't use Cancel buttons in modeless dialog
    boxes. Rather, use Close instead." Round 8, Task 1 chose delayed commit with OK/Cancel, so modal
    is the only sanctioned pairing left. The modality is not declared here — it is ShowDialog(owner),
    in Services/ISettingsLauncher.cs's SettingsLauncher.

    TITLE: 选项 / Options, written by ApplyText() from AppLanguage's "Options" key rather than
    declared here, because AppLanguage has no change notification and a 语言 commit has to re-label
    this window without closing it. PUBLISHED, win-property-win: "Options dialog box titles — Use
    'Options.' Use title-style capitalization. Don't use ending punctuation." Not "TanMenu 设置".

    THE SYSTEM CAPTION, not MainWindow's hand-drawn Aero one. Re-drawing chrome for a second window
    is authoring more of exactly what this round's item 1 strips, and Dialogs/MessageBoxWindow.axaml
    already records the rule this file follows — "a system caption is right for a platform surface."
    CanResize="False" and ShowInTaskbar="False" and no Icon= here. PUBLISHED, win-dialog-box — "Dialog
    boxes don't have title bar icons", "Resizable dialogs can have a Maximize button" (so a fixed one
    has none), "Don't disable the Close button." NONE OF THOSE THREE COME OUT RIGHT FROM THE MARKUP
    ALONE, which was measured rather than assumed: the first build of this window drew an enabled
    Minimize box, a greyed Maximize box and the app's own icon. The code-behind's OnOpened is where
    that is corrected, in Win32, and its remarks carry the measurement and the reason Avalonia's
    SystemDecorations — the design's own suggested remedy — is the wrong tool for it.

    NO ScrollViewer ANYWHERE, and that is a requirement rather than a preference. PUBLISHED,
    win-property-win: "Don't scroll property pages. Instead of using scrollbars, make the property
    pages larger and lay out the pages efficiently." The page this replaced was a ScrollViewer, and
    the long ScrollContentPresenter-padding defect its markup documented goes with it. The one
    ScrollViewer still reachable from this window is inside ToolsCard's per-tool checkbox LIST — a
    control's own scroller, not a property page's — and it is Auto, so it shows no bar at the tool
    counts this app ships. OptionsWindowTests.NoTabHostsAScrollViewer holds the rule on this file.

    SIZE — 711 x 479, RE-MEASURED IN TASK 6. The full method and every number are in the code-behind's
    ApplyText remarks. In short, and unchanged since Task 2: with the window temporarily switched to
    SizeToContent="Height" at Width="711", each tab is selected through UIA's SelectionItemPattern and
    the window's own UIA BoundingRectangle — which for an Avalonia window is the CLIENT rectangle —
    is read after each. The Height is the maximum over the tabs and over both languages.

    THE WIDTH DID NOT MOVE AND THE HEIGHT DID. 711 is the width the settings surface's content pane
    has had since Round 4; Task 2 chose it over the widest tab's 784 DESIRED width for reasons the
    code-behind states, and Task 6 changes nothing about that argument. The Height was 452 — 行为's,
    the tallest under the row-card grammar. Task 6 took the icon, the subtitle and the group box off
    four tabs and split 维护 into two boxes on the fifth, which made 文件夹 the tallest tab by a wide
    margin, so 452 stopped describing this dialog and had to be re-derived rather than kept.

    THE PUBLISHED FLOOR IS CLEARED WITH ROOM. win-property-win: "Support the minimum Windows Vista
    screen resolution of 800 x 600 pixels". At this machine's measured 22 x 56 physical px frame
    (150 %), 711 x 479 client is 725.67 x 516.33 DIP outer. The FIRST cut of Task 6's 文件夹 tab did
    NOT clear it — 577.33 DIP of client is 614.67 outer — and the fix was the published first resort
    rather than the round's fallback: "Don't scroll property pages. Instead of using scrollbars, make
    the property pages larger and lay out the pages efficiently." Four button strips moved beside the
    content they act on instead of onto rows of their own (see FoldersCard.axaml), which took 108.67
    DIP off the tab. The fallback — split 文件夹 in two, six tabs, still under the seven-tab rule —
    was therefore not needed and is still the answer if a later task makes this tab grow again.

    Explicit Width/Height rather than SizeToContent, because a tabbed dialog that resized per tab
    would break PUBLISHED, win-property-win: "Don't assign effects to changing tabs." The measurement
    proves that is not hypothetical: the five tabs want 246 / 350.67 / 468.67 / 294.67 / 198 DIP of
    height in Chinese and 263.33 / 345.33 / 478.67 / 289.33 / 214.67 in English.

    TABS: horizontal, top, FIVE of them, in the page's own order — Round 8, Task 4 took 关于 out into
    Dialogs/AboutWindow.axaml and this strip has held five since. PUBLISHED, win-property-win: "Use
    horizontal tabs if the property window has seven or fewer tabs"; "Use concise tab labels. Use one
    or two words"; "Don't put icons on tabs"; "Don't assign a unique access key" (they cycle with
    Ctrl+Tab); "Otherwise, select the first page by default." The tab index is deliberately NOT
    persisted — "If users are likely to start with the last page displayed, make the page tab
    persist" is conditional and there is no evidence for its antecedent here.

    NOT ONE TabItem CARRIES A LITERAL Header. All five are written by ApplyText() from the same five
    AppLanguage keys the cards' own group heads used to take, and since Round 8, Task 6 THAT IS THE
    ONLY STATEMENT OF A GROUP'S NAME. Task 6 took the group box off every tab whose controls are one
    homogeneous set — 外观, 行为, 常用工具, 更新 — because PUBLISHED, ctrl-group-boxes: "Are all the
    controls inside the group? If so, indicate the relationship on the larger surface, such as the
    parent dialog box or page." This strip IS that larger surface. 文件夹 is the exception: it holds
    four distinct sets (主文件夹 / 数据文件夹 / 缓存 / 备份), so its four INNER boxes stay and each
    carries a head of its own. Deleting a card's GroupHead deleted the assignment behind it, which is
    why Task 2 had to move the header text here first, and
    OptionsWindowTests.EveryTabHeaderComesFromAppLanguage is what keeps a literal from creeping back
    in and leaving a tab unnamed now that nothing else names one.

    WHICH TAB IS THE TALLEST CHANGED HANDS IN TASK 6, and that is the whole reason the Height moved.
    Under the row-card grammar it was 行为 at 452, with 文件夹 at 373. Deleting the icon, the subtitle
    and the group box took 外观 from 345.33 to 246, 行为 from 452 to 350.67, 常用工具 from 320 to
    294.67 and 更新 from 212.67 to 198 — while 文件夹, the one tab that KEEPS its group boxes and gained
    a fourth, went from 373.33 to 468.67. Every other tab therefore has empty space at the bottom now,
    which is what a fixed-size property sheet has on every tab but its tallest, and what the published
    "Don't assign effects to changing tabs" requires it to have.
  -->
  <DockPanel Margin="11">

    <!-- ══ The commit row ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Docked BOTTOM, so the TabControl above it fills whatever is left; a right-aligned single
         row, above nothing else. Every number is PUBLISHED in
         https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout (relative pixels, 9 pt
         Segoe UI at 96 dpi): "Command buttons — 75x23"; "Between buttons — 4 / 7"; "Between
         unrelated controls — 7 / 11" for the gap above the row. The ORDER is PUBLISHED too, stated
         identically in three articles: "Right-align the buttons and use this order (from left to
         right): OK, Cancel, and Apply." OptionsWindowTests.CommitRow_IsOkCancelApplyRightAligned
         reads all of that back off this markup.

         SEMANTICS, all PUBLISHED in win-property-win: 确定 = commit + close; 应用 = commit + stay
         open + re-disable itself; 取消 = discard + close; and the title-bar × is 取消, verbatim —
         "Make sure the Close button on the title bar has the same effect as Cancel or Close. Never
         give it the same effect as OK."

         应用 IS A NAMED DEVIATION and the deviation is booked here rather than argued away.
         PUBLISHED, win-property-win: "Use Apply buttons only with property sheets, not with options
         dialog boxes." PUBLISHED, win-dialog-box: "Don't use Apply buttons in dialog boxes that
         aren't property sheets or control panels." Both are set aside deliberately, because (a) the
         user chose the three-button set, and (b) the guideline that ADMITS Apply is satisfied on its
         own terms — "Provide an Apply button only if the property sheet has settings (at least one)
         with effects that users can evaluate in a meaningful way" — and three of this dialog's
         settings have exactly that: 外观, 字体 and 每列个数 all repaint the launcher behind it.
         Apply is also what gives back the live 外观 preview Task 1's delayed commit removed.

         ACCESS KEYS: none on 确定/取消, "A" on 应用. PUBLISHED, inter-keyboard: "Enter and Esc are
         used for their access keys"; win-property-win on Apply: "Assign 'A' as the access key."
         The marker is in the LABEL, which ApplyText() writes, not here.

         IsDefault / IsCancel are what wire Enter and Esc, and they are declared here because they
         are the buttons' roles, not their text. 应用 opens disabled: PUBLISHED, win-property-win —
         only Apply is "initially disabled" and "enabled when users make a change". It tracks
         SettingsDraft.DirtyChanged rather than being poked by each card, because the cards are in a
         different visual tree from this row. -->
    <StackPanel x:Name="CommitRow" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom"
                Orientation="Horizontal"
                HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                Spacing="7" Margin="0,11,0,0">
      <Button x:Name="OkBtn" Width="75" Height="23" IsDefault="True" Click="OnOkClick" />
      <Button x:Name="CancelBtn" Width="75" Height="23" IsCancel="True" Click="OnCancelClick" />
      <Button x:Name="ApplyBtn" Width="75" Height="23" IsEnabled="False" Click="OnApplyClick" />
    </StackPanel>

    <!-- The tab strip and the page body are entirely the vendored Aero theme's
         (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TabControl.axaml + TabItem.axaml, Round 8 Task 2): no Theme=, no
         class, no local Background or BorderBrush. That is the model this round's item 1 wants and
         Button.tm-update already demonstrates — ride the vendored theme and set nothing it sets.
         TabStripPlacement is left at its default Top, which is the branch both vendored files carry
         their real rules for. -->
    <TabControl x:Name="Tabs">
      <TabItem x:Name="AppearanceTab"><cards:AppearanceCard x:Name="Appearance" /></TabItem>
      <TabItem x:Name="BehaviorTab"><cards:BehaviorCard x:Name="Behavior" /></TabItem>
      <TabItem x:Name="FoldersTab"><cards:FoldersCard x:Name="Folders" /></TabItem>
      <TabItem x:Name="ToolsTab"><cards:ToolsCard x:Name="Tools" /></TabItem>
      <TabItem x:Name="UpdatesTab"><cards:UpdatesCard x:Name="Updates" /></TabItem>
      <!-- THERE WAS A SIXTH, 关于, AND ROUND 8, TASK 4 TOOK IT OUT. It was the only tab with no
           interactive control on it, which is the published test for the surface it belongs on —
           win-property-win: "Is the content solely an application's attributes? If so, use an About
           box." It is Dialogs/AboutWindow.axaml now, opened from the menu band's 关于 item, hosting
           the very same Views/Settings/AboutCard this line used to. -->
    </TabControl>

  </DockPanel>
</Window>
